1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,666
Stop letting your expensive AI write code.

2
00:00:02,666 --> 00:00:03,935
Let it write orders.

3
00:00:03,935 --> 00:00:05,967
Let the cheap one do the typing.

4
00:00:05,967 --> 00:00:07,427
That's the whole trick.

5
00:00:07,427 --> 00:00:08,569
First, what to do.

6
00:00:08,569 --> 00:00:09,204
Then, how.

7
00:00:09,204 --> 00:00:10,410
Then, why it works.

8
00:00:11,210 --> 00:00:11,913
What to do.

9
00:00:11,913 --> 00:00:12,679
Three moves.

10
00:00:12,679 --> 00:00:12,935
One:

11
00:00:12,935 --> 00:00:16,575
keep a tiny root file, CLAUDE dot m d, under sixty lines.

12
00:00:16,575 --> 00:00:16,830
Two:

13
00:00:16,830 --> 00:00:19,449
before any task runs, ask five questions.

14
00:00:19,449 --> 00:00:22,770
If any answer is no, the planner writes it up first.

15
00:00:22,770 --> 00:00:23,153
Three:

16
00:00:23,153 --> 00:00:28,262
a cheap model executes that written order, then runs a check to prove
it worked.

17
00:00:28,262 --> 00:00:29,284
That's the nail.

18
00:00:29,284 --> 00:00:31,583
Everything else is where you put it.

19
00:00:32,384 --> 00:00:32,641
How.

20
00:00:32,641 --> 00:00:34,058
First, lay the boards.

21
00:00:34,058 --> 00:00:35,603
This is the file layout.

22
00:00:35,603 --> 00:00:37,792
The root file points to one index.

23
00:00:37,792 --> 00:00:40,239
The index points to small area sheets.

24
00:00:40,239 --> 00:00:44,811
Long design notes live somewhere else and never get loaded by
accident.

25
00:00:44,811 --> 00:00:47,193
The agents live in dot claude agents.

26
00:00:47,193 --> 00:00:50,026
And one audit script keeps all of it honest.

27
00:00:50,826 --> 00:00:52,599
Here is where the nail goes.

28
00:00:52,599 --> 00:00:53,549
Five questions.

29
00:00:53,549 --> 00:00:55,956
Are the exact files and symbols named?

30
00:00:55,956 --> 00:01:00,453
Is the change so precise that two developers would write the same
diff?

31
00:01:00,453 --> 00:01:02,732
Is there a command that verifies it?

32
00:01:02,732 --> 00:01:04,062
Three files or fewer?

33
00:01:04,062 --> 00:01:05,202
No open decisions?

34
00:01:05,202 --> 00:01:07,419
Five yes, send it to a cheap agent.

35
00:01:07,419 --> 00:01:09,699
Any no, it goes back to the planner.

36
00:01:09,699 --> 00:01:10,966
Never judge by size.

37
00:01:10,966 --> 00:01:13,056
Judge by how well it's specified.

38
00:01:13,855 --> 00:01:16,134
This is the order the planner writes.

39
00:01:16,134 --> 00:01:23,217
Objective, exact files and symbols, the exact change, a verification
command, the expected result, and who does it.

40
00:01:23,217 --> 00:01:25,742
The agent finishes with exactly one word.

41
00:01:25,742 --> 00:01:26,050
DONE.

42
00:01:26,050 --> 00:01:29,623
BLOCKED with a reason, which sends it back to the planner.

43
00:01:29,623 --> 00:01:32,394
Or FAILED twice, which escalates one tier up.

44
00:01:33,194 --> 00:01:33,977
Six workers.

45
00:01:33,977 --> 00:01:36,000
Planner thinks and never codes.

46
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:38,088
Coder types from a locked order.

47
00:01:38,088 --> 00:01:40,763
Mechanic does the boring zero risk edits.

48
00:01:40,763 --> 00:01:44,286
Light handles small fixes that need a bit of judgment.

49
00:01:44,286 --> 00:01:48,331
Deep takes exactly one hard bug, and only with a reproduction.

50
00:01:48,331 --> 00:01:52,181
Research searches many files and gives you five lines back.

51
00:01:52,981 --> 00:01:53,718
How to read.

52
00:01:53,718 --> 00:01:55,437
Never open a big file whole.

53
00:01:55,437 --> 00:01:58,999
Grep for the section you need, then read just those lines.

54
00:01:58,999 --> 00:02:02,069
Roughly a hundred tokens, instead of ten thousand.

55
00:02:02,869 --> 00:02:04,154
Last step of the how.

56
00:02:04,154 --> 00:02:04,826
Lock it in.

57
00:02:04,826 --> 00:02:08,191
The audit script gives you one number, lower is better.

58
00:02:08,191 --> 00:02:11,862
It punishes a fat root file, orphan sheets and broken links.

59
00:02:11,862 --> 00:02:13,758
And the number may never go up.

60
00:02:13,758 --> 00:02:19,509
Wire it into your pre commit hook, your Makefile and your CI, and the
setup can't quietly rot.

61
00:02:20,309 --> 00:02:21,072
Now the why.

62
00:02:21,072 --> 00:02:26,224
Output tokens on top models cost twenty to forty times more than on
entry models.

63
00:02:26,224 --> 00:02:27,878
Planning needs few tokens.

64
00:02:27,878 --> 00:02:29,341
Typing code needs many.

65
00:02:29,341 --> 00:02:34,874
So you pay the expensive rate only for the thinking, and the cheap
rate for the typing.

66
00:02:34,874 --> 00:02:37,418
Your quota lasts, and the quality stays.

67
00:02:38,218 --> 00:02:39,043
Why so small?

68
00:02:39,043 --> 00:02:42,024
The root file is paid for on every single turn.

69
00:02:42,024 --> 00:02:45,958
Big documents rot your context, and the model starts guessing.

70
00:02:45,958 --> 00:02:50,272
One index and small sheets means the model loads only what it needs.

71
00:02:50,272 --> 00:02:52,682
Long design notes stay out of the way.

72
00:02:53,482 --> 00:02:55,433
Why the gate and the statuses?

73
00:02:55,433 --> 00:03:00,244
Routing by readiness stops you wasting expensive runs on half baked
tasks.

74
00:03:00,244 --> 00:03:04,146
Deep only accepts a reproduction, so no costly rabbit holes.

75
00:03:04,146 --> 00:03:07,137
Explicit statuses mean nothing fails silently.

76
00:03:07,137 --> 00:03:10,453
The audit ratchet means the setup only gets better.

77
00:03:10,453 --> 00:03:15,200
And the same index works for Claude and for Gemini, using GEMINI dot m
d.

78
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:17,084
So, start today.

79
00:03:17,084 --> 00:03:18,778
Write the thin root file.

80
00:03:18,778 --> 00:03:20,608
Add the five question gate.

81
00:03:20,608 --> 00:03:23,387
Hand your first real task to the planner.

82
00:03:23,387 --> 00:03:27,656
Every template, copy and paste ready, is in scaffold dot p d f.

83
00:03:27,656 --> 00:03:28,266
Go build.

